tongxin-100:
**Key idea:** `assign clk_out = sel ? clk1 : clk0;` switches *whenever* `sel` changes, so if `sel` toggles while the old clock is high and the new one is low (or mid-pulse), `clk_out` gets a **runt pulse / truncated period** that can violate flop min-pulse-width and corrupt state. Fix: a **glitch-free clock mux** — each clock is gated by an enable that is synchronized to *that* clock, changes only while that clock is **low**, and is interlocked so the new clock is enabled only after the old one is fully off.

### The glitch
```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk0",            "wave": "hhllhhllhhllhhll" },
  { "name": "clk1",            "wave": "llllllhhhhhhllll" },
  { "name": "sel",             "wave": "0....1.........." },
  { "name": "sel ? clk1 : clk0", "wave": "hhllhlhhhhhhllll" }
],
  "head": { "text": "sel flips mid-way through a clk0 high phase: half-width runt pulse, then clk1" } }
```

### Glitch-free version
```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk0",    "wave": "hlhlhlhlhlhlhlhlhlhlhlhl" },
  { "name": "clk1",    "wave": "hhhlllhhhlllhhhlllhhhlll" },
  { "name": "sel",     "wave": "01......................" },
  { "name": "en0",     "wave": "1....0.................." },
  { "name": "en1",     "wave": "0..............1........" },
  { "name": "clk_out", "wave": "hlhlhlllllllllllllhhhlll" }
],
  "head": { "text": "en0 falls on a clk0 negedge, both off, en1 rises on a clk1 negedge" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
// Glitch-free mux for two asynchronous clocks.
// Each enable: 2 posedge sync flops + 1 negedge flop so it only changes while its clock is low.
module gf_clk_mux (
  input  logic clk0, clk1,
  input  logic rst_n,     // async reset, deassert synchronized per domain in real design
  input  logic sel,       // 0 -> clk0, 1 -> clk1 (may be asynchronous)
  output logic clk_out
);
  logic s0a, s0b, en0;    // clk0 domain
  logic s1a, s1b, en1;    // clk1 domain

  // clk0 side: allowed when sel==0 and clk1 path is fully off
  always_ff @(posedge clk0 or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) {s0b, s0a} <= '0;
    else        {s0b, s0a} <= {s0a, ~sel & ~en1};
  always_ff @(negedge clk0 or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) en0 <= 1'b0;
    else        en0 <= s0b;

  // clk1 side: allowed when sel==1 and clk0 path is fully off
  always_ff @(posedge clk1 or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) {s1b, s1a} <= '0;
    else        {s1b, s1a} <= {s1a, sel & ~en0};
  always_ff @(negedge clk1 or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) en1 <= 1'b0;
    else        en1 <= s1b;

  // In silicon: use library ICG/AND + OR (or a dedicated glitch-free mux cell), don't let synthesis restructure
  assign clk_out = (clk0 & en0) | (clk1 & en1);
endmodule
```

### Explanation
- **Break-before-make**: `en1` can only rise after `en0` is 0 (and vice versa), so both clocks are never gated through simultaneously.
- Enables change on the **negative edge** of their own clock: the AND output is already low, so the gate cannot chop a high pulse.
- The cross-coupled enables cross clock domains, hence the synchronizer flops.
- Switching latency ≈ 2–3 cycles of the old clock + 2–3 cycles of the new clock; `clk_out` is held low in between.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- If the **old clock is stopped**, `en_old` never clears and the switch never completes — needs a forced-switch path (reset of that enable) or a requirement that both clocks run during switching.
- STA: define generated clocks for each mux input on `clk_out`, set them `-logically_exclusive`/`physically_exclusive`, and constrain the enable paths.
- Avoid letting synthesis merge/re-time the gating logic; instantiate cells or apply `dont_touch`.

### Follow-ups
- *Synchronous (related) clocks?* One flop per side (negedge) suffices since no metastability concern.
- *How to verify?* Glitch/min-pulse checker on `clk_out` with `$realtime`, random `sel` toggles at random phases, and stopped-clock scenarios.

tongxin-103:
**Key idea:** An APB slave performs its operation **once**, at the completion of the access phase (`PSEL && PENABLE && PREADY`). Map a DATA register (write = push, read = pop) and a STATUS register; with an array-based FIFO the head is available combinationally, so `PREADY` can be tied high and `PRDATA` driven during the access phase. Report illegal operations (push when full, pop when empty, unmapped address) with `PSLVERR`.

### Register map
| Offset | Name | Write | Read |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0x0 | DATA | push PWDATA | pop, returns head |
| 0x4 | STATUS | ignored + PSLVERR | `{count, full, empty}` |

