### Code
```systemverilog
// (1) Structural master-slave using NAND-based D latches (gate primitives)
module dlatch_g (input wire d, en, output wire q, qn);
  wire dn, s_n, r_n;
  not  u0 (dn, d);
  nand u1 (s_n, d,  en);
  nand u2 (r_n, dn, en);
  nand u3 (q,  s_n, qn);       // SR latch with feedback
  nand u4 (qn, r_n, q);
endmodule

module dff_ms (input wire clk, d, output wire q);
  wire clk_n, m, m_n, q_n;
  not      u_inv (clk_n, clk);
  dlatch_g u_m (.d(d), .en(clk_n), .q(m), .qn(m_n));   // master
  dlatch_g u_s (.d(m), .en(clk),   .q(q), .qn(q_n));   // slave
endmodule

// (2) UDP: edge-sensitive sequential primitive
primitive udp_dff (q, clk, d);
  output q; reg q;
  input  clk, d;
  table
  // clk   d  : q : q+
     r     0  : ? : 0 ;     // rising edge (01) captures d
     r     1  : ? : 1 ;
     (0x)  ?  : ? : - ;     // keep value on uncertain edges (simplification)
     f     ?  : ? : - ;     // falling edge: hold
     ?     *  : ? : - ;     // d changes without clock edge: hold
  endtable
endprimitive

// (3) Library cell (technology-specific name)
// DFFQX1 u_ff (.CK(clk), .D(d), .Q(q));
```

### Explanation
- Mux-style latches written as `assign m = en ? d : m;` simulate as latches but synthesize to a **combinational loop** and suffer a static hazard when `en` switches with `d = m = 1` (needs the consensus term `d & m`). Gate-level SR latches or real latch cells are safer.
- The inverter on `clk` creates a short window where both latches may be transparent if delays are unbalanced (race-through); real cells use two-phase or carefully sized transmission gates.
- Synthesis tools may not recognize hand-built flops for timing (no setup/hold arcs) — this is an academic answer; production RTL uses `always_ff` or cell instantiation.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Without reset the structural flop powers up X in simulation (fine; add reset gating if required).
- UDP rows for `(x1)`/`(0x)` define pessimism; omitting them gives X on uncertain edges.

### Follow-ups
- *Negative-edge flop?* Swap which latch gets `~clk`.
- *Why is a latch alone not a flop?* It is transparent for the whole level, so data can race through multiple stages in one phase.

